# MELEJI HOMESTAYS – PERMANENT PROJECT RULES

These rules apply to all implementation, content, refactoring and maintenance tasks.

Read the existing code and project documentation before making changes.

Never compromise responsiveness, readability, accessibility or performance to achieve visual effects.

## 1. BRAND AND DESIGN CONSISTENCY

- Meleji Homestays must remain the dominant brand.
- Maintain the approved premium, authentic, warm and modern design direction.
- Use the centralized design tokens.
- Do not invent alternative palettes on individual pages.
- Do not introduce random font families.
- Use consistent button heights, border radii, spacing and typography.
- Preserve visual hierarchy.
- Avoid unnecessary decorative elements.
- Use gradients deliberately and consistently.
- Avoid flat, overly saturated section backgrounds where subtle brand gradients are more appropriate.
- Preserve neutral space around important content.
- Maintain both desktop and mobile design quality.

## 2. COPYWRITING RULES

Never use generic AI marketing clichés.

PROHIBITED PHRASES:
- "It's not just ... it's ..."
- "More than just ..."
- "Whether you're ... or ..."
- "Embark on a journey ..."
- "Discover the magic ..."
- "Unlock unforgettable ..."
- "Nestled in the heart of ..."
- "Where luxury meets ..."
- "Where comfort meets ..."
- "A hidden gem ..."
- "Your gateway to ..."
- "Experience like never before ..."
- "Something for everyone ..."
- "Unforgettable memories await ..."
- "Seamless experience ..."
- "Breathtaking adventure awaits ..."

Avoid repetitive marketing adjectives.

Do not repeat the same message in hero sections, cards, supporting text and footers.

Prefer specific, natural and credible hospitality copy.

Write for human travelers, not search-engine stuffing.

Do not use excessive em dashes.

Prefer commas, periods, colons or en dashes where grammatically appropriate.

Never replace every punctuation mark with an en dash.

Avoid repeated exclamation marks.

Avoid excessive title-case capitalization.

Do not fabricate:
- Testimonials.
- Ratings.
- Review counts.
- Awards.
- Certifications.
- Partner relationships.
- Accommodation amenities.
- Service guarantees.
- Safari availability.
- Exact travel durations.
- Confirmed business prices.
- Foundation registrations.
- Children's personal stories.

If information is unknown, record it in docs/content-status.md.

Avoid publishing visible text such as:
- Lorem ipsum.
- Coming soon.
- Sample content.
- Amazing experience.
- Your text here.
- Dummy description.

For unfinished sections, use a clean content state or keep them unpublished until real information is ready.

## 3. BUTTON RULES

All main CTA buttons must share the same design system.

Use the existing button component.

Avoid one-off button CSS.

BUTTON STATES:
- Default.
- Hover.
- Focus-visible.
- Active.
- Disabled.
- Loading, only if required.

Maintain WCAG AA text contrast where applicable.

Target at least 4.5:1 contrast for ordinary text.

Before and after gradient fill transitions, text must remain readable.

No transparent text on a similarly colored background.

No low-contrast gold on cream or pale text on light gradients.

For sliding-label animations:
- Duplicate text only as a visual animation layer.
- Expose one accessible button label.
- Prevent clipping of important text.
- Keep both animated labels readable.
- Use stable dimensions.
- Do not create layout shifts.
- Do not let pseudo-elements cover text unexpectedly.
- Preserve visible keyboard focus.

Do not apply desktop hover effects as the only feedback on touchscreens.

Main booking CTA must remain visible in the mobile header.

## 4. IMAGE QUALITY RULES

Never use CSS scaling to compensate for an undersized source image.

Never enlarge low-resolution images excessively.

Use images appropriate to their rendered size and expected device pixel ratio.

Use aspect-ratio and object-fit carefully.

Avoid distorted faces, buildings, wildlife and landscape photography.

CARD HOVER:
Preferred scale: 1.025–1.04.
Default: 1.035.
Do not exceed 1.05 without explicit approval.

Do not apply scale transforms to the entire card if only the image should zoom.

Use overflow-hidden media containers.

Avoid image blur filters during transitions.

Avoid strong brightness, saturation and contrast manipulation.

Use meaningful image crops.

Implement responsive source handling where available.

Use width and height or aspect ratio to reserve image space.

Prioritize hero imagery.

Lazy-load noncritical images.

Do not repeatedly reuse a single unrelated stock image across different destinations.

Do not assume an external image URL will remain available.

Preserve original high-quality assets.

Never invent proof that a photograph represents an actual Meleji property or service.

## 5. MOBILE-FIRST RESPONSIVENESS

Start with narrow layouts and progressively enhance.

Check these widths:
320, 360, 375, 390, 430, 768, 1024, 1280 and 1440px.

Avoid fixed widths that break on small screens.

Use clamp(), min(), max(), CSS Grid and Flexbox where suitable.

No unintended horizontal scrolling.

No overlapping navigation controls.

No clipped headings.

No CTA text overflow.

No oversized empty spaces.

No tiny links or unusable form controls.

Keep interactive targets comfortably touch-friendly, preferably at least 44 x 44 CSS pixels.

Account for fixed or sticky header height.

Do not let anchored section headings hide under the header.

Use appropriate scroll-margin-top.

Avoid desktop-only interaction assumptions.

Mobile interactions must be deliberate, not simply smaller desktop components.

## 6. NAVIGATION RULES

Exactly three primary desktop navbar links:
Stay, Explore, Our Story.

Main CTA:
Book Your Stay.

Hamburger must remain present on all viewport sizes.

The hamburger contains the full site navigation.

On narrow screens, hide the three main links before they collide with the logo or CTA.

Preserve:
- Accessible menu button.
- aria-expanded.
- aria-controls.
- Escape-key support.
- Focus management.
- Scroll locking.
- Working navigation links.
- Smooth open/close behavior.

Close the menu after selecting a route.

No menu content hidden behind fixed elements.

No accidental stacking-context conflicts.

No animation that prevents users from navigating.

## 7. ICON SYSTEM

Prefer Font Awesome Free icons for standard interface symbols.

Use the official Font Awesome package or consistent verified assets.

Do not mix incompatible icon versions.

Do not use placeholder Unicode symbols as permanent interface icons.

Use icons semantically.

Examples:
- Flight: fa-plane
- Safari: fa-binoculars
- Location: fa-location-dot
- Accommodation: fa-bed
- Coffee: fa-mug-hot
- Food: fa-utensils
- Walking: fa-person-walking
- Hiking: fa-person-hiking
- Community: fa-hand-holding-heart

Verify icon availability in the installed Font Awesome version.

Do not treat all requested icons as available in the free package without verification.

Icon animation must be subtle.

Avoid excessive rotations and bouncing.

Keep line weight and icon sizing visually consistent.

## 8. ANIMATION RULES

Use CSS transitions for straightforward interaction.

Use IntersectionObserver only where scroll reveals require it.

Do not install a heavy animation framework without a documented reason.

Do not animate layout-affecting properties unnecessarily.

Prefer opacity and transform.

Avoid excessive will-change usage.

Avoid continuous background animations.

Respect prefers-reduced-motion.

No scroll hijacking.

No artificial delayed page loading.

No forced preloader.

No arbitrary setTimeout delays for visual effect.

No pinned sections that trap scrolling or break touch navigation.

Avoid excessive z-index values.

## 9. PERFORMANCE RULES

Use Vite's optimized production build.

Do not introduce React or other frameworks without approval.

Keep dependencies minimal.

Avoid importing entire icon libraries unnecessarily.

Avoid large videos as default backgrounds.

Avoid enormous image files.

Use appropriate modern image compression.

Minimize layout shift.

Avoid excessive DOM nesting.

Do not duplicate event listeners.

Remove unused CSS and JavaScript.

Do not use JavaScript for effects CSS can handle.

Maintain responsive page rendering under ordinary mobile network conditions.

Do not confuse a loading animation with actual performance optimization.

## 10. SHARED COMPONENT RULES

Never duplicate navbar markup independently on every page.

Never duplicate large footer sections independently.

Maintain two footer variants:
- Full Footer.
- Compact Footer.

Use reusable components for:
- Buttons.
- Room cards.
- Safari cards.
- Destination cards.
- Experience cards.
- Forms.
- Breadcrumbs.
- CTA sections.

Use centralized variables and component modifiers.

Avoid creating a new card type when an existing type can be safely extended.

Do not make unrelated components depend on fragile CSS selectors.

Avoid overly broad selectors that accidentally affect other pages.

Do not write page-specific styles that unexpectedly override global components.

## 11. ROUTING AND FILE STRUCTURE

Use organized directories.

Do not scatter every HTML page in the project root.

Avoid broken relative links.

Avoid case-sensitive path mistakes.

Do not use absolute local filesystem paths.

Do not hardcode development-server URLs.

Ensure direct navigation to each public page works in production.

Maintain a consistent route strategy.

Do not silently replace real routes with # links.

Avoid broken image paths on nested routes.

Keep production compatibility with static cPanel hosting.

## 12. CONTENT AND DATA MODELING

Keep destination data separate from safari package data.

Do not hardcode multiple safari packages inside one large HTML block.

Prepare reusable data models.

Every destination can have:
- Name.
- Slug.
- Region.
- Description.
- Hero image.
- Gallery.
- Activities.
- Related packages.

Every safari package can have:
- Name.
- Slug.
- Destination references.
- Duration.
- Itinerary.
- Inclusions.
- Exclusions.
- Price or enquiry status.
- Gallery.

Real package content will be supplied later.

Do not invent official itineraries.

Do not display unfinished cards as real products.

Allow future content imports without rewriting the entire UI.

## 13. FORMS AND BOOKING

Use accessible labels.

Validate required fields.

Use appropriate input types.

Show clear validation errors.

Never claim successful delivery when no backend is connected.

Do not collect card details without an appropriate secure payment provider.

Do not expose secrets in frontend code.

Provide clear user feedback.

Keep the booking experience simple on mobile.

Never use dead-end buttons.

If an interaction cannot yet be completed, report that limitation instead of inventing functionality.

## 14. SECURITY AND PRIVACY

Never place API secrets in public frontend files.

Avoid unsafe HTML insertion.

Sanitize or safely render user-generated content.

Use appropriate form protections when backend services are introduced.

Do not expose personal data or children's information unnecessarily.

Use privacy-conscious analytics if introduced later.

Do not use photos of vulnerable children without appropriate permission.

## 15. ACCESSIBILITY AND SEO

Use semantic landmarks.

Maintain logical heading structure.

Provide accessible image alternatives.

Decorative images should have empty alt attributes.

Use meaningful link labels.

Support keyboard interaction.

Use visible focus states.

Respect reduced-motion settings.

Use unique page titles and meta descriptions.

Avoid duplicate meta content.

Use canonical URLs when configured.

Use structured data only when supported by verified business facts.

## 16. PREVENT COMMON AI AGENT ERRORS

Before changing code:
- Read the relevant existing files.
- Identify the current component conventions.
- Check for existing reusable solutions.
- Understand the dependencies affected.

Avoid:
- Rewriting unrelated working code.
- Creating duplicate CSS rules.
- Creating conflicting media queries.
- Importing missing packages.
- Referencing nonexistent assets.
- Creating fake links.
- Introducing unrequested page redesigns.
- Adding unnecessary new dependencies.
- Creating desktop layouts that fail on mobile.
- Leaving temporary debugging code in production.
- Silently ignoring warnings and errors.
- Describing untested interactions as fully tested.

Do not replace a good solution simply to make the code look different.

Fix root causes instead of layering additional CSS overrides.

## 17. VERIFICATION AFTER EVERY PHASE

Inspect package.json scripts.

Use npm run dev for local preview.

Run npm run build.

Run npm run preview where appropriate.

Inspect console and terminal errors.

Check missing assets.

Check navigation behavior.

Check mobile overflow.

Check button contrast.

Check image quality.

Check accessibility basics.

Check animations under reduced-motion settings.

Check that changes have not broken existing pages.

If browser inspection is not available, clearly say so.

Never claim a test passed unless it was actually executed.

## 18. CHANGE DISCIPLINE

Work on the requested phase only.

Preserve approved designs.

Explain significant architecture changes.

Do not silently modify the brand identity.

Do not change official text, pricing or company claims without instruction.

Update documentation when architecture or design standards change.

Complete and validate one phase before starting another.

Always prioritize:
Clarity → Functionality → Responsiveness → Accessibility → Performance → Visual refinement.
